    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Role in tetanus:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " benzos = the standard of care for rigidity and spasms; diazepam helps control seizures, rigidity and sedation; dosing needs meticulous titration and close observation; midazolam (water soluble) is preferred for prolonged tx: ↓ lactic acidosis from the propylene glycol solvent in diazepam and lorazepam; benzos may not prevent reflex spasms: paralytics and mechanical ventilation may be needed."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Inj CI:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " acute narrow-angle glaucoma, sleep apnea syndrome; severe resp insuff (unless mechanically ventilated); intra-arterial inj (arteriospasm → gangrene)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
